<section id="current_privacy">
    <p>At Simbiat Software, one of our main priorities is the privacy of our visitors. This Privacy Policy document contains types of information that is collected and recorded by Simbiat Software and how we use it.</p>
    <p>If you have additional questions or require more information about our Privacy Policy, do not hesitate to contact us using any means provided on Our <a href="/about/contacts" target="_blank">Contacts</a> page.</p>
    <p>This Privacy Policy applies only to our online activities and is valid for visitors to our website in regard to the information that they shared and/or collect in Simbiat Software. This policy is not applicable to any information collected offline or via channels other than this website.</p>
    <h2>Consent</h2>
    <p>By using our website, you acknowledge this Privacy Policy. Where our processing relies on consent, we will request it separately and explicitly.</p>
    <h2>Information we collect</h2>
    <p>The personal information that you are asked to provide, and the reasons why you are asked to provide it, will be made clear to you at the point we ask you to provide your personal information. However, the only mandatory information is your email address and username during initial registration.</p>
    <p>If you contact us directly, we may receive additional information about you such as your name, email address, the contents of the message and/or attachments you may send us, and any other information you may choose to provide. Contents of the messages sent to you from us can also be stored up to a year.</p>
    <p>When you register for an Account, we ask only for a username and email. However, later you can provide other contact information, including items such as name, company name, address, other email addresses, telephone number, and/or social network profiles.</p>
    <p>When an image is being uploaded to the service, by default it may be converted, and extra metadata from the original file (ICC, IPTC, EXIF, XMP, etc.) can be lost, but in a case of certain formats, like SVG, the uploaded file may retain potentially sensitive information, which is not intended for storage by the website. It is advised to double-check the images being uploaded.</p>
    <h2>How we use your information</h2>
    <p>We use the information we collect in various ways, including to:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Understand and analyze how you use our website</li>
        <li>Improve, personalize, and expand our website</li>
        <li>Develop new products, services, features, and functionality</li>
        <li>Communicate with you through emails or other channels in regard to critical information or personalized subscriptions</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Log Files</h2>
    <p>Simbiat Software follows a standard procedure of using log files (access logs). These files log visitors when they visit websites. All hosting companies do this and a part of hosting services' analytics. The information collected by log files may include internet protocol (IP) addresses, browser type, date and time stamp, referring/exit pages, and possibly some other information. These are not linked to any information that is personally identifiable. The logs are used to help identify malicious activities with the help of security tools (<a href="https://www.crowdsec.net" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">CrowdSec</a>) and debugging in case of some errors. These logs normally stay on the server for several days due to rotation logic.</p>
    <h2>Cookies and Web Beacons</h2>
    <p>Like any other website, Simbiat Software uses 'cookies'. These cookies are used <strong>ONLY</strong> to store authentication information. Personalized settings may be stored in server-side sessions or your browser's local storage.</p>
    <p>For more general information on cookies, please read <a href="https://www.privacypolicyonline.com/what-are-cookies/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">"What Are Cookies"</a>.</p>
    <p>Emails received from Simbiat Software can have a Web Beacon used to check if the respective email was read and show the respective small image in the email footer. This Beacon has a unique ID linking the email to the respective notification in the database. No data is collected through this Beacon itself aside from the fact that the email was read. However, the Beacon initiates a connection to the webserver, which results in writing of data in log files, as described in the previous section. Regardless of that, it is allowed to block it, if so desired. If the Beacon is blocked, the respective notification may be shown as unread on the website.</p>
    <h2>Client Hints</h2>
    <p>Simbiat Software can process <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Client_hints" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Client Hints</a> if respective HTTP headers are sent by the browser or browser-like software being used to connect to the service. The information from these headers is used to identify potential bots and otherwise malicious entities. Since the information sent through these headers can contain items used to identify users, it is not stored by the service. But due to the way it is being processed, it may be temporarily saved in log files, as described in the previous section.</p>
    <h2>Third Party Privacy Policies</h2>
    <p>Simbiat Software's Privacy Policy does not apply to other services. Thus, we are advising you to consult the respective Privacy Policies of these third-party services for more detailed information. It may include their practices and instructions about how to opt-out of certain options. That said, Simbiat Software is committed to not using third-party services that can collect and/or process user data. Any intentional data sharing with such third-parties is disclosed as part of this privacy policy.</p>
    <p>You can choose to disable cookies through your individual browser options. To know more detailed information about cookie management with specific web browsers, it can be found at the browsers' respective websites.</p>
    <h2>IPs and User-Agents</h2>
    <p>As per the nature of the Internet, Simbiat Software gets your IP address and User-Agent (browser details) when you connect to the service. Both can be used to help identify you, but Simbiat Software limits the use and storage of this information to only certain circumstances:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Log files, as described above. Processed under legitimate interest for security purposes.</li>
        <li>Database table of banned IP addresses, which will store IP addresses that were identified as malicious and need to be prevented from accessing the site. This database table will have unobfuscated IP addresses, date each of them was added, and the reason it was added, without any other information. Processed under legitimate interest for security purposes.</li>
        <li>Sessions database table can have both IP and User-Agent. Neither is obfuscated since session data can be used to help identify malicious activity. But each session is normally removed after 5 minutes of inactivity. If you are a registered user, this data will also be used to help you identify your sessions across all your devices and optionally remove sessions that you do not recognize. Processed under legitimate interest for session security and user-facing session management.</li>
        <li>Cookie database table can have both IP and User-Agent. This table stores "remember me" cookies that are created when you log in while enabling the respective optional checkbox. There is no personal information stored in cookies' data, and each cookie is normally removed after 30 days of not being used. The information is used to help you identify your "remember me" cookies across all your devices and optionally remove cookies that you do not recognize. Processed under legitimate interest for session security and user-facing session management.</li>
        <li>Search Engine Optimization (SEO) database tables can have information derived from IP (country, city) and User-Agent (client and operating system versions), but original values are not stored. Some data can have accompanying unique visit ID, but neither that ID nor the data can be linked to a user. Some data can be treated as anonymized access logs (as described above), collected for analytical purposes only. Data is normally stored for no more than 1 year. Processed under legitimate interest for anonymous analytics.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA)</h2>
    <p>Simbiat Software does not sell any user data and as such is compliant with CCPA.</p>
    <h2>GDPR Data Protection Rights</h2>
    <p>We would like to make sure you are fully aware of all of your data protection rights. Every user is entitled to the following rights:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>To access – You have the right to request copies of your personal data.</li>
        <li>To rectification – You have the right to request that we correct any information you believe is inaccurate. You also have the right to request that we complete the information you believe is incomplete.</li>
        <li>To erasure – You have the right to request that we erase your personal data, under certain conditions. If no such request is raised, the data collected by the service will be retained until the account is active or an otherwise justified purge of data is required or requested. Some types of data can be purged periodically as described in other sections of this privacy policy.</li>
        <li>To restrict processing – You have the right to request that we restrict the processing of your personal data, under certain conditions.</li>
        <li>To object to processing – You have the right to object to our processing of your personal data, under certain conditions.</li>
        <li>To data portability – You have the right to request that we transfer the data that we have collected to another organization, or directly to you, under certain conditions.</li>
        <li>To lodge a complaint – You have the right to lodge a supervisory authority complaint if you consider that the processing of data infringes the Regulation. Complaint can be lodged in the Member State of your habitual residence, place of work, or place of the alleged infringement.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Self-service erasure can be done using the respective <a href="/uc/removal">page</a> once logged in.</p>
    <p>Please note that self-service erasure will not remove the user profile itself if a "soft" erasure is requested. "Hard" erasure will remove the profile details but will not remove the user-generated data like "posts" in the "Talks" part of this website, or any of the uploaded files, but all registered personal identification information will be removed.</p>
    <p>If you also require removing all the user-generated content created under your profile, please, make the respective request in <a href="/talks/sections/5">Support</a>. If you make a request, we have one month to respond to you. When processing your request, the administration will evaluate if there is any information that you have previously shared that may be beneficial to other users of the website, that can be retained (with obfuscation) should you agree to that.</p>
    <p>If you would like to exercise any of these rights, please contact us. If you have previously requested "soft" erasure, you can also request profile restoration, if required.</p>
    <h2>Children's Information</h2>
    <p>Another part of our priority is adding protection for children while using the internet. We encourage parents and guardians to observe, participate in, and/or monitor and guide their online activity.</p>
    <p>Simbiat Software does not knowingly collect any Personal Identifiable Information from children under the age specified by the respective privacy act or other law. For example, under Finnish law and CCPA this means 13, and under GDPR in jurisdictions that have not lowered the default threshold – 16. If you think that your child provided this kind of information on our website, we strongly encourage you to contact us immediately, and we will do our best efforts to promptly remove such information from our records.</p>
</section>
